---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LeadCapture from '../components/LeadCapture.astro';
const title = 'ADA Violation Damages Calculator | adaviolationcalc.com';
const description = 'Estimate potential ADA violation damages for employment discrimination, public accommodation, and other ADA claims. Free, informational calculator covering back pay, compensatory, and punitive damages.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>ADA Violation Damages Calculator</h1>
  <p><em>Published by The Click Lab Agency LLC. Last reviewed September 2026. Not legal advice.</em></p>
  <p>The Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) prohibits discrimination against people with disabilities in employment (Title I), state and local government services (Title II), public accommodations (Title III), and telecommunications. When an employer or covered entity violates the ADA, the injured party may recover a range of damages depending on the type of claim and the defendant. This calculator estimates potential recovery for the most common ADA claims using the statutory remedies for each Title, the employer-size caps in 42 U.S.C. &sect; 1981a, and a few simplifying assumptions explained on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.</p>

  <h2>Run the estimate</h2>
  <form id="calc" novalidate>
    <label for="claim-type">Type of ADA claim</label>
    <select id="claim-type">
      <option value="employment-termination">Title I — Wrongful termination / failure to accommodate</option>
      <option value="employment-hiring">Title I — Failure to hire / promote</option>
      <option value="employment-harassment">Title I — Disability-based harassment</option>
      <option value="public-accommodation">Title III — Public accommodation denial</option>
      <option value="government">Title II — Government services denial</option>
    </select>
    <label for="annual-income">Annual income at time of violation (USD)</label>
    <input id="annual-income" type="number" value="55000" min="0" />
    <label for="months-out">Months of lost employment (if applicable)</label>
    <input id="months-out" type="number" value="6" min="0" max="120" />
    <label for="employer-size">Employer size (employees)</label>
    <select id="employer-size">
      <option value="15-100">15–100 employees</option>
      <option value="101-200">101–200 employees</option>
      <option value="201-500">201–500 employees</option>
      <option value="500+">500+ employees</option>
    </select>
    <label for="willful">Was the violation willful or egregious?</label>
    <select id="willful">
      <option value="no">No / unclear</option>
      <option value="yes">Yes — documented bad faith or repeated violations</option>
    </select>
    <button type="submit">Estimate damages</button>
  </form>
  <p id="result" aria-live="polite"></p>

  <h2>Understanding your estimate</h2>
  <p>The range shown represents a plausible window of outcomes for cases with similar facts, not a guaranteed outcome in your case. ADA litigation results depend heavily on the quality of your documentation, whether you engaged in the accommodation process, whether the employer&rsquo;s stated reasons for any adverse action hold up to scrutiny, and how your damages are calculated at trial. Consult an experienced employment attorney for a case-specific evaluation.</p>
  <p>The estimate does not include attorney fees, which are separately recoverable by the prevailing plaintiff under 42 U.S.C. &sect; 12205 and paid by the defendant. In many ADA cases, attorney fees awarded at trial or in settlement negotiations significantly exceed the capped compensatory and punitive damages — making fee-shifting a critical driver of employer settlement decisions even in lower-dollar-value cases.</p>

  <h2>How ADA damages are calculated</h2>
  <p>ADA Title I (employment) damages mirror those available under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act. They include back pay (lost wages from violation to resolution), front pay (future lost earnings if reinstatement isn&rsquo;t feasible), compensatory damages (emotional distress, out-of-pocket costs), and punitive damages for willful violations. Combined compensatory and punitive damages are capped by employer size under 42 U.S.C. &sect; 1981a(b)(3).</p>
  <p>Title II claims against state and local governments allow injunctive relief and, where the discrimination was intentional (most courts require deliberate indifference), compensatory damages; punitive damages are not available (<em>Barnes v. Gorman</em>, 536 U.S. 181 (2002)). Title III claims against private businesses are different: a private plaintiff can obtain an injunction ordering the business to fix the barrier or policy, plus attorney fees, but no money damages under federal law. Money damages for public accommodation claims come from state laws where they exist &mdash; California&rsquo;s Unruh Civil Rights Act, for example, provides at least $4,000 per violation &mdash; or from civil penalties in U.S. Department of Justice enforcement actions.</p>

  <h2>Employer-size damages caps (Title I)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>15–100 employees:</strong> $50,000 combined cap on compensatory + punitive damages.</li>
    <li><strong>101–200 employees:</strong> $100,000 cap.</li>
    <li><strong>201–500 employees:</strong> $200,000 cap.</li>
    <li><strong>500+ employees:</strong> $300,000 cap.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Back pay and front pay are <em>not</em> subject to these caps &mdash; they are equitable remedies that can be as large as the facts support. Attorney fees are also recoverable separately and are not counted against the cap. For high-earning employees at large employers, back pay and front pay often far exceed the capped compensatory/punitive component.</p>

  <h2>The reasonable accommodation requirement</h2>
  <p>Most ADA employment claims center on the employer&rsquo;s failure to provide a reasonable accommodation. When an employee requests an accommodation for a disability, the employer is obligated to engage in an &ldquo;interactive process&rdquo; — a good-faith dialogue to identify an accommodation that allows the employee to perform the essential functions of the job without imposing undue hardship on the employer. Failure to engage in the interactive process, denial of a clearly reasonable accommodation, or adverse action shortly after an accommodation request are the most common bases for ADA claims. Damages analysis starts from the date of the accommodation denial or the adverse employment action.</p>

    <h2>What each ADA Title allows</h2>
  <div style="overflow-x:auto">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Title</th><th>Who it covers</th><th>What you can recover</th><th>Cap or limit</th><th>First step and deadline</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Title I &mdash; employment</td><td>Employers with 15+ employees</td><td>Back pay, front pay or reinstatement, compensatory and punitive damages, attorney fees</td><td>Compensatory + punitive capped at $50,000&ndash;$300,000 by employer size; no punitive damages against government employers</td><td>EEOC charge within 180 days, or 300 days where a state or local agency also enforces disability law</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Title II &mdash; state and local government</td><td>All state and local government programs and services</td><td>Injunctive relief; compensatory damages for intentional discrimination; attorney fees</td><td>No punitive damages</td><td>Complaint to the relevant federal agency or the Department of Justice, or a lawsuit; the deadline borrows your state&rsquo;s analogous limitations period</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Title III &mdash; private businesses open to the public</td><td>Stores, restaurants, hotels, doctors&rsquo; offices, websites in many courts</td><td>Injunction ordering the barrier or policy fixed; attorney fees</td><td>No money damages for private plaintiffs under federal law; state laws may add them</td><td>No agency charge needed; a DOJ complaint is optional</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <p>These examples use round numbers and show how the Title and the employer&rsquo;s size change the result.</p>

  <h3>Example 1 &mdash; Failure to accommodate at a small employer</h3>
  <p>A dispatcher earning $52,000 asks a company with 80 employees for a modified schedule to attend dialysis. The company refuses to discuss it and fires her a month later. She is out of work for 10 months: back pay &asymp; $43,333. A jury values her emotional distress at $120,000, but for a 15&ndash;100 employee employer the combined compensatory and punitive cap is $50,000. Total: about $93,333 plus attorney fees.</p>

  <h3>Example 2 &mdash; Large employer, termination with evidence of malice</h3>
  <p>An analyst earning $70,000 at a company with 1,200 employees is fired after disclosing an epilepsy diagnosis, and emails show a manager calling him &ldquo;a liability.&rdquo; He is out of work 12 months (back pay $70,000), and the court awards six months of front pay ($35,000). The jury awards $200,000 in compensatory and $400,000 in punitive damages; the cap for employers over 500 cuts the combined $600,000 to $300,000. Total: $405,000 plus attorney fees.</p>

  <h3>Example 3 &mdash; Public accommodation, two different states</h3>
  <p>A wheelchair user cannot enter a retail store because of a step at the only entrance. Under Title III, a federal court can order the store to install a ramp and pay her attorney fees, but it cannot award her money damages. If the store is in California, the Unruh Civil Rights Act adds statutory damages of at least $4,000 for each occasion she was denied access. The same facts in a state without a comparable law produce a fixed entrance and paid legal fees, but no check.</p>

  <h2>Where to learn more</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-ada-damages-are-calculated/">how ADA damages are calculated</a>, <a href="/guides/types-of-ada-violations/">types of ADA violations</a>, <a href="/guides/what-to-do-after-ada-violation/">what to do after an ADA violation</a>, and <a href="/guides/common-ada-claim-misconceptions/">common ADA claim misconceptions</a>. Read the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> for common questions about EEOC deadlines, disability definitions, and the reasonable accommodation standard.</p>

  <h2>What this calculator does not do</h2>
  <p>It does not decide whether you have a disability under the ADA&rsquo;s definition, whether an accommodation was reasonable, or whether it would have caused the employer undue hardship &mdash; those questions decide most cases. Its compensatory starting points ($40,000 for harassment, $25,000 for other employment claims, $10,000 for government-services claims) and its 1.5&times; punitive multiplier are illustrative assumptions chosen by the publisher, not averages from verdict data. It does not subtract earnings from a new job, add interest or lost benefits, or model state disability laws, which in many states have no cap. For Title III public accommodation claims it shows no money damages, because federal law provides none to private plaintiffs.</p>

  <h2>Where to get help</h2>
  <p>For employment claims, file a charge with the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) or your state fair employment agency; it is free and does not require a lawyer, but the 180- or 300-day deadline is strict. For government services or public accommodations, you can file a complaint with the U.S. Department of Justice Civil Rights Division at ada.gov. The Job Accommodation Network (askjan.org) offers free, confidential guidance on accommodations to employees and employers alike. Employment and disability rights attorneys often take strong cases on contingency because the ADA awards fees to a prevailing plaintiff. If you would like The Click Lab to pass your details to an attorney in its network, the optional form below does that; it is a disclosed referral product, and our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains exactly what is shared.</p>

  <LeadCapture caseType="ada_violation" caseTypeLabel="ADA Violation" />


  <script>
    document.getElementById('calc').addEventListener('submit', e => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const type = document.getElementById('claim-type').value;
      const income = parseFloat(document.getElementById('annual-income').value) || 55000;
      const months = parseFloat(document.getElementById('months-out').value) || 0;
      const size = document.getElementById('employer-size').value;
      const willful = document.getElementById('willful').value === 'yes';
      const caps = {'15-100':50000,'101-200':100000,'201-500':200000,'500+':300000};
      const cap = caps[size];
      const backPay = (income / 12) * months;
      const frontPay = type.startsWith('employment') ? income * 0.5 : 0;
      const compBase = type === 'employment-harassment' ? 40000 : (type.startsWith('employment') ? 25000 : (type === 'government' ? 10000 : 0));
      const punitive = willful && type.startsWith('employment') ? compBase * 1.5 : 0;
      const cappedCP = Math.min(compBase + punitive, cap);
      const total = backPay + frontPay + cappedCP;
      const fmt = n => '$' + Math.round(n).toLocaleString();
      document.getElementById('result').innerHTML =
        `<strong>Estimated range: ${fmt(total * 0.7)} &ndash; ${fmt(total * 1.3)}</strong><br>Back pay: ${fmt(backPay)} &bull; Front pay: ${fmt(frontPay)} &bull; Compensatory/punitive (capped): ${fmt(cappedCP)}<br><em>Attorney fees recoverable separately. Caps apply to compensatory + punitive only. Consult an employment attorney for case-specific analysis.</em>` + (type === 'public-accommodation' ? '<br><strong>Title III note:</strong> federal law gives private plaintiffs an injunction and attorney fees, not money damages. Any money recovery depends on your state&rsquo;s law.' : '');
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
